l is interesting because while it averages 494μs across 141 calls, there's huge variance with some instances taking 13.8ms, suggesting certain calls process significantly more data than others.\n\nLooking at the FoldEval rounds, they handle layers where rem_n stays below the PrecomputeM threshold, so early layers work with large datasets (2^21 elements) while later ones shrink down to 2^15 elements. Since each layer depends on the previous one's output, I can't parallelize across layers with multi-streaming.\n\nThe bit_rev_frac_build_k2 kernel takes about 8ms per call across 6 instances and appears to fuse bit-reversal with tree construction for two layers at once. I'm wondering if fusing more layers together could speed this up, but I should examine the kernel more closely before deciding on an optimization strategy.\n\nActually, there's another angle worth considering: the MLE sumcheck rounds. With n_max = log2(stacked_height) - l_skip, and at APC 300 where n_max equals n_stack, reducing the number of rounds by 2 would save roughly 11ms. But n_stack is a protocol parameter, not an implementation detail, so I can't change it without altering the protocol itself.\n\nI need to settle on a concrete optimization approach. The most promising direction appears to be overlapping Round 0 z\n\n-check evaluation with the tail end of GKR's inner rounds on background streams. But wait—that won't work because Round 0 needs challenges from the transcript that includes GKR observations, so there's a dependency I can't bypass.\n\nLet me think about this differently: what if I pre-compute the Round 0 work items and GPU buffer allocations while GKR's inner rounds are still running? The CPU work of building work items, computing buffer sizes, and pre-allocating GPU buffers doesn't actually depend on GKR's output—it only needs the AIR structure, which is already known.\n\nThe GPU evaluation itself does depend on GKR output for the transcript challenges, but the prep work could happen in parallel. Looking at the numbers, Round 0 prep takes roughly 15-31ms across 312 AIRs per segment, and that serial CPU work before launching threads is the real bottleneck. A previous optimization that overlapped this precomputation saved 64ms, so there's real potential here.\n\nI'm wondering if there are other serial operations between GKR completion and Round 0 that could be moved earlier—things like work item construction that only depend on AIR structure, not GKR output itself. But I need to look at the actual code flow more carefully to see what's really blocking what. Actually, I'm thinking about overlapping the initial NTT computation of the RS code matrix across segments, though that might be handled at a higher level outside of this component.\n\nLet me step back and look at stacked_reduction, which is taking 75ms at APC 300 and shows no scaling improvement at all—that's a red flag for a design issue. But realistically, no single optimization is going to get me to the 239ms target. I need to think about what combination of incremental improvements is actually achievable. Looking at my options, overlapping Round 0 CPU prep with GKR's inner rounds could save 20-30ms with relatively low complexity and no risk to VPMM sensitivity, though the actual savings might be smaller if CPU prep is already fast.\n\nOptimizing the RS code matrix NTT is another angle—the forward transform is full-width, so there might be a more efficient layout—but that requires understanding the NTT implementation better and carries medium complexity. Pipelining WHIR's inner rounds by overlapping fold with NTT could squeeze out 10-20ms, though the gains per round might be modest since WHIR is only 129ms total. The zero-row optimization for Merkle tree hashing looks promising at 15-25ms potential savings—many stacked trace rows are just padding, so I could precompute the hash of an all-zero row once and reuse it.\n\nThe key insight is that the sponge hash absorbs elements sequentially, so