{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011gMYeTB3ivz2uUxcNUJ4A6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01J1PSSGwTrH8SjJVxPqzM2B","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Review revised optimization plan","prompt":"Review this implementation plan following the review guideline. Return your response in the format: Findings, Improvements, Assessment.\n\nYou MUST read and follow the review standard in:\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt/review_guideline.md`\n\nAlso read:\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt/context.md` (project context)\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md` (the task being planned)\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-1430-column-batched-ntt-for-rs-code-matrix/plan.md` (the plan to review)\n\nThen verify the plan's claims against the actual codebase:\n\n1. Read `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs` function `rs_code_matrix` (lines 181-279) and confirm the current NTT call matches what the plan describes.\n2. Read `crates/cuda-backend/src/ntt.rs` to confirm NttImpl is accessible, step scheduling logic, and the batch_ntt function signature.\n3. Read `crates/cuda-common/src/d_buffer.rs` to confirm DeviceBuffer::non_owning exists and has the expected signature.\n4. Check that the NTT CUDA FFI (`crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/ntt.rs`) uses the raw pointer from DeviceBuffer, confirming that a non-owning sub-view will pass the correct offset pointer.\n5. Verify the plan's claim about log_trace_height values at APC 300. Look for how log_codeword_height is computed in rs_code_matrix and what the actual stacked height would be.\n6. Check whether NttImpl is pub(crate) or private — the new function needs to construct it.\n7. Verify that column-NTTs are truly independent (the CUDA kernel doesn't have cross-column data dependencies).\n\nPay special attention to:\n- Whether the non-owning DeviceBuffer sub-view is safe (no concurrent access, no use-after-free)\n-