{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RPZRshQ18KjPgSnibYUJ48","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BSuk7fN33nTf9aFjd9wJ1S","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-1430-column-batched-ntt-for-rs-code-matrix/plan.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11093,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11093,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01J1PSSGwTrH8SjJVxPqzM2B","session_id":"b4e113b8-a396-45cc-8e86-654a475f0e59","uuid":"2fb694e7-09e0-4169-9f15-f3aed421ba8b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BSuk7fN33nTf9aFjd9wJ1S","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Column-Batched NTT for RS Code Matrix\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tImprove the NTT throughput in `rs_code_matrix` by splitting the column-wise forward NTT into L2-cache-sized batches. At APC 300, the NTT processes ~53K columns × 2^14 height simultaneously, creating a 3.4GB working set that dwarfs the 72MB L2 cache. NTT butterfly stages at large strides (2^7–2^13 elements) read from DRAM on every access, achieving ~30% of peak bandwidth. By processing ~1000 columns per batch (~64MB, fitting in L2), the second NTT kernel step (stages 8–14) reads data that's still L2-resident from the first step (stages 1–7), roughly doubling effective bandwidth for those stages. APC 300 benefits disproportionately: its 64KB columns fit well in L2 per batch, while APC 0's 4MB columns see less benefit.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### `rs_code_matrix` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs`, lines 181-279\n10\t\n11\tThe function computes Reed-Solomon codewords for the stacked trace matrix:\n12\t\n13\t1. **Lines 197-226**: `batch_expand_pad` or `stack_traces_into_expanded` + optional `batch_ntt_small` — prepares the codeword buffer with stacked data, expanded to codeword height.\n14\t2. **Lines 236-252**: Optional `mle_interpolate_stages` — subset-zeta transform for `l_skip > 0`.\n15\t3. **Lines 254-264**: `bit_rev` — in-place bit-reversal permutation of all columns.\n16\t4. **Lines 267-274**: `batch_ntt` — forward NTT, the **most expensive step** (~47ms per segment at APC 300).\n17\t\n18\t### `batch_ntt` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/ntt.rs`, lines 87-130\n19\t\n20\t```rust\n21\tpub fn batch_ntt(buffer, log_trace_height, log_blowup, width, bit_reverse, is_intt) {\n22\t    let padded_poly_size = 1 << (log_trace_height + log_blowup);\n23\t    if bit_reverse { ntt::bit_rev(buffer, buffer, ...); }\n24\t    let mut _impl = NttImpl::new(buffer, log_trace_height, padded_poly_size, width, is_intt);\n25\t    // For log_trace_height in 11..=17 (APC 300 case, ~14):\n26\t    let step = log_trace_height / 2;\n27\t    _impl.step(step + log_trace_height % 2);  // kernel launch 1: stages 0..7\n28\t    _impl.step(step);                          // kernel launch 2: stages 7..14\n29\t}\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\tEach `_impl.step(iterations)` calls `ntt::ct_mixed_radix_narrow` (`crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/ntt.rs`, line 141) which is an FFI call to `_ct_mixed_radix_narrow`. The CUDA kernel receives:\n33\t- `d_inout`: raw device pointer (`DeviceBuffer::as_mut_raw_ptr()`)\n34\t- `poly_count`: number of independent column-NTTs = `width`\n35\t- `padded_poly_size`: elements per column\n36\t- Stage/radix parameters\n37\t\n38\tThe kernel processes all `poly_count` columns in parallel. Each column's NTT is independent.\n39\t\n40\t### Per-segment numbers at APC 300\n41\t\n42\t- `width` ≈ 53K columns\n43\t- `codeword_height` = 2^14 = 16384 (`log_trace_height` ≈ 14, after `l_skip` correction; `log_blowup` = 0 since expansion was pre-applied)\n44\t- Column size: 16384 × 4 bytes = 64 KB\n45\t- Total data: 53K × 64KB = 3.4 GB\n46\t- L2 cache: 72 MB on RTX 4090\n47\t- Columns per L2 batch: floor(64MB / 64KB) ≈ 1000 columns\n48\t- NTT kernel launches per batch: 2 (since 14 ≤ 17 → two steps)\n49\t\n50\t### Per-segment numbers at APC 0\n51\t\n52\t- `width` ≈ 784 columns\n53\t- `codeword_height` = 2^20 = 1048576\n54\t- Column size: 1048576 × 4 = 4 MB\n55\t- Total data: 784 × 4MB = 3.1 GB\n56\t- Columns per L2 batch: floor(64MB / 4MB) = 16 columns\n57\t- NTT kernel launches per batch: 3 (since 20 ≤ 30 → three steps)\n58\t- Number of batches: ceil(784/16) = 49\n59\t\n60\t## Changes\n61\t\n62\t### Change 1: Add `batch_ntt_column_batched` helper in `crates/cuda-backend/src/ntt.rs`\n63\t\n64\tAdd a new function alongside `batch_ntt` that splits the NTT into L2-sized column batches:\n65\t\n66\t```rust\n67\t/// Like `batch_ntt`, but processes columns in L2-cache-sized batches for better\n68\t/// memory locality. Falls back to `batch_ntt` when the total working set already\n69\t/// fits in L2.\n70\tpub fn batch_ntt_column_batched(\n71\t    buffer: &DeviceBuffer<F>,\n72\t    log_trace_height: u32,\n73\t    log_blowup: u32,\n74\t    width: u32,\n75\t    bit_reverse: bool,\n76\t    is_intt: bool,\n77\t) {\n78\t    if log_trace_height == 0 {\n79\t        return;\n80\t    }\n81\t    let padded_poly_size = 1u64 << (log_trace_height + log_blowup);\n82\t    let col_bytes = padded_poly_size as usize * std::mem::size_of::<F>();\n83\t    let total_bytes = col_bytes * width as usize;\n84\t\n85\t    // If the entire working set fits in ~60MB (leaving headroom in the 72MB L2),\n86\t    // there's no benefit to batching — use the standard path.\n87\t    const L2_BUDGET: usize = 60 * 1024 * 1024;\n88\t    if total_bytes <= L2_BUDGET {\n89\t        batch_ntt(buffer, log_trace_height, log_blowup, width, bit_reverse, is_intt);\n90\t        return;\n91\t    }\n92\t\n93\t    let batch_cols = (L2_BUDGET / col_bytes).max(1) as u32;\n94\t\n95\t    // Bit-reverse entire buffer first (one pass) — the batched NTT steps\n96\t    // expect bit-reversed input within each column.\n97\t    if bit_reverse {\n98\t        unsafe {\n99\t            ntt::bit_rev(\n100\t                buffer,\n101\t                buffer,\n102\t                log_trace_height,\n103\t                padded_poly_size as u32,\n104\t                width,\n105\t            )\n106\t            .unwrap();\n107\t        }\n108\t    }\n109\t\n110\t    // Process NTT in column batches\n111\t    for batch_start in (0..width).step_by(batch_cols as usize) {\n112\t        let batch_width = (width - batch_start).min(batch_cols);\n113\t        let element_offset = batch_start as usize * padded_poly_size as usize;\n114\t        // SAFETY: offset is within buffer bounds; non-owning view is valid for\n115\t        // the duration of the NTT call; NTT operates only within the view.\n116\t        let sub_buffer = unsafe {\n117\t            DeviceBuffer::non_owning(\n118\t                buffer.as_ptr().add(element_offset) as *mut F,\n119\t                batch_width as usize * padded_poly_size as usize,\n120\t            )\n121\t        };\n122\t        let mut ntt_impl = NttImpl::new(\n123\t            &sub_buffer,\n124\t            log_trace_height,\n125\t            padded_poly_size as u32,\n126\t            batch_width,\n127\t            is_intt,\n128\t        );\n129\t        // Replicate the same step schedule as batch_ntt\n130\t        if log_trace_height <= 10 {\n131\t            ntt_impl.step(log_trace_height);\n132\t        } else if log_trace_height <= 17 {\n133\t            let step = log_trace_height / 2;\n134\t            ntt_impl.step(step + log_trace_height % 2);\n135\t            ntt_impl.step(step);\n136\t        } else if log_trace_height <= 30 {\n137\t            let step = log_trace_height / 3;\n138\t            let rem = log_trace_height % 3;\n139\t            ntt_impl.step(step);\n140\t            ntt_impl.step(step + (if log_trace_height == 29 { 1 } else { 0 }));\n141\t            ntt_impl.step(step + (if log_trace_height == 29 { 1 } else { rem }));\n142\t        } else if log_trace_height <= 40 {\n143\t            let step = log_trace_height / 4;\n144\t            let rem = log_trace_height % 4;\n145\t            ntt_impl.step(step);\n146\t            ntt_impl.step(step + (if rem > 2 { 1 } else { 0 }));\n147\t            ntt_impl.step(step + (if rem > 1 { 1 } else { 0 }));\n148\t            ntt_impl.step(step + (if rem > 0 { 1 } else { 0 }));\n149\t        }\n150\t    }\n151\t}\n152\t```\n153\t\n154\t**Why this helps**: Within each batch, the 2 NTT kernel steps both operate on ~64MB of data. After the first step writes results to L2, the second step finds them still L2-resident. The effective bandwidth for the second step jumps from ~300 GB/s (DRAM, with strided access) to ~2-3 TB/s (L2). Since the second step does roughly half the total work, the expected overall speedup is ~1.5-2x.\n155\t\n156\t**Note**: The `NttImpl` struct and its `step` method are already defined in `ntt.rs` (lines 32-77) and are `pub(crate)` accessible. The `ensure_initialized(is_intt)` call happens inside `NttImpl::new`.\n157\t\n158\t### Change 2: Use `batch_ntt_column_batched` in `rs_code_matrix`\n159\t\n160\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs`, lines 254-274\n161\t\n162\tReplace:\n163\t```rust\n164\tunsafe {\n165\t    bit_rev(&codewords, &codewords, log_codeword_height as u32, codeword_height as u32, width as u32)?;\n166\t}\n167\tbatch_ntt(&codewords, log_codeword_height as u32, 0u32, width as u32, false, false);\n168\t```\n169\t\n170\tWith:\n171\t```rust\n172\tbatch_ntt_column_batched(\n173\t    &codewords,\n174\t    log_codeword_height as u32,\n175\t    0u32,\n176\t    width as u32,\n177\t    true,   // let the batched function handle bit_rev\n178\t    false,\n179\t);\n180\t```\n181\t\n182\tThis also eliminates the separate `bit_rev` call (it's done inside `batch_ntt_column_batched`), keeping the code cleaner. The bit_rev is NOT batched — it still runs on all columns at once. The only batched operation is the NTT kernel steps.\n183\t\n184\t**Why bit_rev is not batched**: Bit-reversal is a simple scatter/gather with no reuse between elements. L2 caching provides no benefit because each element is read once and written once. The strided access pattern for bit_rev is inherent to the permutation. Batching it would add kernel launch overhead without improving bandwidth.\n185\t\n186\t## Invariants\n187\t\n188\t1. **Mathematical correctness**: Each column's NTT is independent. Processing columns in batches of 1000 produces the same result as processing all 53K at once — the NTT butterfly operations within each column only access elements of that same column.\n189\t\n190\t2. **Memory safety**: The `non_owning` DeviceBuffer view points into the middle of the `codewords` buffer. It's only used within the for-loop body and dropped before the next iteration. The `codewords` buffer outlives all views.\n191\t\n192\t3. **APC 0 behavior**: At APC 0, `total_bytes = 784 × 4MB = 3.1GB > 60MB`, so batching IS activated. But with `batch_cols = 16`, there are 49 batches × 3 steps = 147 kernel launches (vs 3 currently). The additional kernel launch overhead is 147 × ~10μs = 1.5ms. The L2 benefit for 4MB columns is moderate — large-stride butterflies (stride > 1MB) exceed L2 even within a batch. Expected net effect: neutral to slightly positive.\n193\t\n194\t4. **Kernel launch overhead**: At APC 300, 53 batches × 2 steps = 106 kernel launches (vs 2 currently). Additional launch overhead: ~1ms. The L2 bandwidth benefit (~30ms) far exceeds this overhead.\n195\t\n196\t5. **No VPMM interaction**: The optimization only changes how existing NTT kernels are called — no new allocations, no buffer layout changes, no pool state disruption.\n197\t\n198\t## Measurement Plan\n199\t\n200\t1. Run `run_pairing.sh` for APC 0, 100, 300 before and after.\n201\t2. Primary metric: `STARK (excl. trace)` at APC 300 — expect 20-50ms improvement.\n202\t3. Secondary metric: `Trace Commit` breakdown — expect the NTT portion to decrease.\n203\t4. APC 0 regression check: STARK excl trace within ±20ms.\n204\t5. Optionally sweep `L2_BUDGET` values (32MB, 48MB, 64MB) to find the optimal batch size.\n205\t\n206\t## Rollback Criteria\n207\t\n208\t- Less than 15ms improvement on STARK excl trace at APC 300.\n209\t- Regression > 20ms on STARK excl trace at APC 0.\n210\t- Proof verification failure at any APC configuration.\n211\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01J1PSSGwTrH8SjJVxPqzM2B","session_id":"b4e113b8-a396-45cc-8e86-654a475f0e59","uuid":"06368fa5-9f81-4b1f-9a76-addf7989ba41","timestamp":"2026-04-16T13:26:02.822Z"}
